    "definitionOfDone": "ASSIGNMENT: L03 PERSONAL BRANDING (DEFINING YOUR USP)\nCOURSE: 8175 Sports Entertainment and Event Marketing\nCOMPETENCIES ASSESSED: 8175-64, 8175-107\n\nTHE TASK (what the student was asked to do)\nTitle: Assignment: Defining Your Personal Brand (The CEO of YOU)\n\nDescription:\n\nIt is important to understand your own personal brand. When you understand who you are and what you stand for, you can find new opportunities and ways to strengthen your weaknesses. A brand statement also helps others understand who you are, what you are great at, and how you differentiate yourself from others.\n\nThe Task:\n\nAnswer the questions below to create your personal brand statement. Be honest and dig deep.\n\n1. Your Value\n\n- What value can you provide the world (or people) with your strengths and attributes?\n\n2. Your USP (Unique Selling Proposition)\n\n- What is your Unique Selling Proposition?\n- What is the one thing that makes you stand out from the crowd? Why should a team pick you over the 100 other people applying for the job?\n\n3. Your Audience\n\n- Who do you want to help, serve, or would benefit the most from what you can offer?\n\nWhat your work needs to show:\n\n- I can position myself like a product by stating my USP, explaining why a team should pick me over 100 other applicants, and naming who would benefit most (required)\n- I can identify my personal strengths by naming my strengths and attributes and explaining what value they let me give to people\n\nYou can also show:\n\n- I can plan my career path by connecting my brand statement to the kind of job or team I want and showing how my strengths could get me there\n- I can explain branding by pulling my value, USP, and audience together into a personal brand statement that sounds like me and shows what I stand for\n\nSubmission:\n\nSubmit your answers in a text entry box or a Google Doc.\n\nPRIMARY COMPETENCY (Milestone 1; must be MET)\n- 8175-64 Explain the role of product positioning.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * as perceived by the customer\n    * relative to its competitors\n    * a discussion of the push-pull method\n\nSECONDARY COMPETENCIES (must be at least PARTIALLY MET)\n- 8175-107 Identify personal skills and interests applicable to the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * individual personal skills (e.g., leadership ability, problem-solving ability, organizational ability)\n    * interests (e.g., sports, theater, music, physical fitness, recreational activities, volunteer activities)\n    * networking with local business representatives\n    * volunteering or working in sports, entertainment, and event industries to gauge one’s interest in the business of sports, entertainment, and events and to gain valuable work experience\n\nTERTIARY COMPETENCIES (never required; name any the work clearly shows under ALSO SHOWN)\n- 8175-12 Demonstrate career- and life-management skills.\n- 8175-56 Explain branding.\n\nTEACHER GRADING NOTES\nDo not grade for \"correct\" answers, as this is personal. Grade for effort and completion.\n\n- Did they answer all three sections?\n- Is the \"Unique Selling Proposition\" specific or descriptive? Give higher points for descriptive answers.\n\nPASSING RULE: APPROVED when Milestone 1 (the primary competency) is MET and Milestones 2, 3 and 4 are each MET or PARTIALLY MET. Tertiary competencies never affect the result.\n\nPASSING STANDARD\nThe work does every part of the task the assignment asks for, in the student's own words. Specific examples, not general statements. Judge each competency only by what this assignment asks the student to do. The lines under a competency are examples of what evidence can look like, not a checklist: one specific, well-done example that fits the task is enough, and a part of a competency the task does not ask for is never required."
