    "definitionOfDone": "ASSIGNMENT: L04 PERSONAL BRAND ANALYSIS (PUBLIC FIGURES)\nCOURSE: 8175 Sports Entertainment and Event Marketing\nCOMPETENCIES ASSESSED: 8175-56, 8175-105, 8175-2\n\nTHE TASK (what the student was asked to do)\nTitle: Assignment: Personal Brand Analysis (The Celebrity Case Study)\n\nDescription:\n\nEverybody has a personal brand. Whether you realize it or not, your actions mold your personal brand. You will be taking a closer look at a public figure's personal brand to see how they manage their reputation and career.\n\nThe Task:\n\nAnswer the following questions about the individual's personal brand. Please write in complete sentences.\n\n1. The Subject\n\n- Who is the public figure you chose?\n- What makes this individual well known?\n\n2. The SWOT Analysis\n\n- What is a major Strength this individual has?\n- What is a Weakness or challenge they face?\n- What is an Opportunity they could use to grow their brand?\n- What is a Threat that could damage their brand?\n\n3. The Public Perception\n\n- How do others describe this person?\n- Does their public reputation match who they really seem to be?\n\n4. The Core Drivers\n\n- What do you think motivates this individual?\n- What do you think they are passionate about outside of their job?\n\nWhat your work needs to show:\n\n- I can explain a personal brand by describing what makes my public figure well known and how others describe them, so a reader gets a clear picture (required)\n- I can identify the parts of a SWOT analysis by giving one strength, one weakness, one opportunity, and one threat for my public figure, each in a complete sentence\n- I can think critically by deciding whether my public figure's reputation matches who they really seem to be and backing up my answer with reasons from what I found\n\nYou can also show:\n\n- I can explain branding in sports and entertainment by showing how my public figure's personal brand connects to their sport, show, or industry and grows their career\n- I can analyze the effects of negative media by explaining how the threat I named could spread through news or social media and hurt my public figure's brand\n\nSubmission:\n\nSubmit your responses in a text entry box.\n\nPRIMARY COMPETENCY (Milestone 1; must be MET)\n- 8175-56 Explain branding.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * brand personality\n    * brand name\n    * brand mark\n    * trade name\n    * trade character\n    * trademark\n    * brand tagline\n\nSECONDARY COMPETENCIES (must be at least PARTIALLY MET)\n- 8175-105 Identify the components of a SWOT analysis.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * strengths\n    * weaknesses\n    * opportunities\n    * threats\n- 8175-2 Demonstrate critical thinking and problem-solving.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * recognizing and analyzing problems\n    * evaluating potential solutions and resources\n    * using a logical approach to make decisions and solve problems\n    * implementing effective courses of action\n\nTERTIARY COMPETENCIES (never required; name any the work clearly shows under ALSO SHOWN)\n- 8175-57 Explain the concept of branding as it relates to sports, entertainment, and event marketing.\n- 8175-86 Analyze the effects of negative media.\n\nTEACHER GRADING NOTES\nLook for depth in the \"Motivation\" and \"How others describe this person\" answers. The \"Strengths and Weaknesses\" section is a good check for critical thinking.\n\nPASSING RULE: APPROVED when Milestone 1 (the primary competency) is MET and Milestones 2, 3 and 4 are each MET or PARTIALLY MET. Tertiary competencies never affect the result.\n\nPASSING STANDARD\nThe work does every part of the task the assignment asks for, in the student's own words. Specific examples, not general statements. Judge each competency only by what this assignment asks the student to do. The lines under a competency are examples of what evidence can look like, not a checklist: one specific, well-done example that fits the task is enough, and a part of a competency the task does not ask for is never required."
