    "promptText": "Title: Assignment: The \"Rule of Six\" Slide Challenge\n\nDescription:\n\nMost PowerPoint presentations fail because the presenter treats the slides like a document. They fill the screen with text and read it to the audience. This is called \"Death by PowerPoint.\" To be effective, you must follow the design rules used by professional marketers.\n\nThe Rules:\n\n- The Rule of Six: No more than six lines of text per slide. No more than six words per line.\n- Images: No more than 2 images per slide.\n- The KISS Principle: Keep It Simple. Light background, dark font. Minimal animations.\n\nThe Task:\n\nYou will create a short slide deck (3 to 5 slides) on a topic of your choice.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- The Script: You must write a script for what you will say in the \"Speaker Notes\" section.\n- The Visuals: Your slides must strictly follow the Rule of Six.\n- Design: Use a high contrast design (Light background, dark font).\n- Communication Types: In your speaker notes, label one place where you use verbal communication, one where you use nonverbal (a gesture or eye contact) and one where you use visual (an image or chart).\n\nWhat your work needs to show:\n\n- I can present like a professional by building a 3 to 5 slide deck that follows the Rule of Six, with speaker notes that add to my slides (required)\n- I can identify types of communication by labeling in my speaker notes one place I use verbal, one I use nonverbal, and one I use visual communication\n- I can use presentation software well by making high-contrast slides in PowerPoint or Google Slides with a light background, dark font, no more than 2 images, and minimal animations\n\nYou can also show:\n\n- I can speak clearly to an audience by writing my speaker notes the way I would actually say them out loud, in clear words that fit my audience\n\nSubmission:\n\nSubmit your Slide Deck (PowerPoint or Google Slides link) with the Speaker Notes included.",
