    "definitionOfDone": "ASSIGNMENT: L09 DEFINING BRANDING (THE MENTOR CHALLENGE)\nCOURSE: 8175 Sports Entertainment and Event Marketing\nCOMPETENCIES ASSESSED: 8175-56, 8175-54, 8175-21\n\nTHE TASK (what the student was asked to do)\nTitle: Assignment: What IS Branding? (The Mentor Challenge)\n\nDescription:\n\nWe have spent the year looking at logos, slogans, and the emotional connections companies make with consumers. Now, it is time to synthesize that information.\n\nThe Scenario:\n\nImagine that a younger student, or perhaps a relative with absolutely no business background, comes up to you and asks: \"I keep hearing people talk about 'branding,' but I don't get it. What actually IS branding?\"\n\nThe Task:\n\nStep into the role of the teacher. Write out your response to this person (Minimum 250 words).\n\nStructure:\n\n- The Introduction: Start by telling them what branding is not. Define it simply.\n- The Examples: Use a real example (like Apple or Coca Cola). Explain *why* people buy that brand.\n- The Promise: Explain how a brand is a promise to the customer. That trust is branding.\n- The Conclusion: Wrap it up by telling them why it matters.\n\nWhat your work needs to show:\n\n- I can explain branding by saying what it is not, defining it simply, explaining why people buy a real brand like Apple, and showing how a brand is a promise (required)\n- I can translate business language into everyday words by explaining branding so someone with no business background understands it, including any business term I use\n- I can write a clear explanation of at least 250 words with an introduction, an example, the promise, and a conclusion, checked for spelling and grammar\n\nYou can also show:\n\n- I can think critically about brands by explaining the real reasons people choose my example brand over others instead of only describing it\n- I can explain branding in sports, entertainment, or events by using an example from one of them to show how a brand builds trust with fans\n\nSubmission:\n\nSubmit your response as a Text Entry.\n\nPRIMARY COMPETENCY (Milestone 1; must be MET)\n- 8175-56 Explain branding.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * brand personality\n    * brand name\n    * brand mark\n    * trade name\n    * trade character\n    * trademark\n    * brand tagline\n\nSECONDARY COMPETENCIES (must be at least PARTIALLY MET)\n- 8175-54 Translate technical language into commonly understood language.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * understanding specialized vocabulary\n    * analyzing the audience\n    * expressing technical terms and concepts in everyday language\n- 8175-21 Demonstrate reading and writing skills.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * reading and interpreting workplace documents\n    * ability to convey messages with clarity\n    * professional tone, appropriate to audience\n    * grammar\n    * forms and conventions (e.g., formatting documents, using an email signature)\n\nTERTIARY COMPETENCIES (never required; name any the work clearly shows under ALSO SHOWN)\n- 8175-2 Demonstrate critical thinking and problem-solving.\n- 8175-57 Explain the concept of branding as it relates to sports, entertainment, and event marketing.\n\nTEACHER GRADING NOTES\nUse the provided rubric structure for speed grading:\n\n- Clarity (20 pts): Did they move beyond just saying \"it is a logo\"?\n- The Promise (Critical Check): Look for the section where they define branding as a \"promise\" or \"trust.\"\n\nPASSING RULE: APPROVED when Milestone 1 (the primary competency) is MET and Milestones 2, 3 and 4 are each MET or PARTIALLY MET. Tertiary competencies never affect the result.\n\nPASSING STANDARD\nThe work does every part of the task the assignment asks for, in the student's own words. Specific examples, not general statements. Judge each competency only by what this assignment asks the student to do. The lines under a competency are examples of what evidence can look like, not a checklist: one specific, well-done example that fits the task is enough, and a part of a competency the task does not ask for is never required."
