    "definitionOfDone": "ASSIGNMENT: L16 THE PRICE IS RIGHT (PRICING STRATEGIES)\nCOURSE: 8175 Sports Entertainment and Event Marketing\nCOMPETENCIES ASSESSED: 8175-19, 8175-67, 8175-95\n\nTHE TASK (what the student was asked to do)\nTitle: Assignment: The Pizza Break-Even Challenge\n\nDescription:\n\nSetting a price isn't just guessing. It is a mathematical balance between covering your costs and what the customer is willing to pay.\n\nThe Task:\n\nYou are opening a concession stand at the football stadium selling Pizza. You need to determine your price.\n\nPart 1: The Math (Break-Even Analysis)\n\n- Data: Fixed Costs: 500 dollars. Variable Cost (per pizza): 5 dollars.\n- Scenario A: You sell the pizza for 10 dollars. How many pizzas must you sell to cover your 500 dollar rent? (Show your work).\n- Scenario B: You lower the price to 7 dollars. How many pizzas must you sell *now* to cover your rent?\n\nPart 2: The Strategy\n\n- Question: Which pricing strategy would you use for a *new* pizza brand?\n\n- Price Skimming: Setting a high price to look exclusive.\n- Market Penetration: Setting a low price to get people to try it.\n- Justification: Explain your choice in 2 sentences.\n- Bundle It: Create a combo (for example, a slice and a drink) and give it a price. Explain why a fan would buy the bundle instead of the pizza alone.\n- The Rest of the Mix: Price is one of the Ps. In one sentence each, describe your stand's product, place and promotion.\n\nWhat your work needs to show:\n\n- I can calculate a break-even point by showing my work to find how many pizzas I must sell to cover the 500 dollar rent at 10 and at 7 dollars (required)\n- I can choose a pricing strategy by picking price skimming or market penetration for my new pizza brand and justifying my choice in 2 sentences\n- I can explain bundling by creating a combo, giving it a price, and explaining why a fan would buy the bundle instead of the pizza alone\n\nYou can also show:\n\n- I can identify the Ps of the marketing mix by describing my stand's product, place, and promotion in one sentence each\n- I can explain a pricing decision by using my break-even numbers or what fans will pay to show why my chosen price makes sense\n- I can explain stadium concessions by showing how my stand's combo or setup fits what fans want to buy at the football stadium\n- I can explain competition by showing how my price or combo would help my stand win fans over other food choices at the stadium\n\nSubmission:\n\nSubmit your calculations and strategy as a Text Entry.\n\nPRIMARY COMPETENCY (Milestone 1; must be MET)\n- 8175-19 Apply mathematical skills to job-specific tasks.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * performing calculations (e.g., percentages, fractions, addition, subtraction, averages, measurement, conversions, monetary transactions)\n    * applying mathematical processes to accomplish job-specific tasks (e.g., estimating required supplies, completing expense reports)\n    * managing personal finance (e.g., understanding wage rates, paycheck deductions, taxes, sales receipts)\n\nSECONDARY COMPETENCIES (must be at least PARTIALLY MET)\n- 8175-67 Identify pricing strategies as they relate to the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * group sales\n    * personal seat license (PSL) promotions\n    * discounts\n    * prestige pricing\n    * odd-even pricing\n    * promotional pricing\n    * price lining\n    * tiered pricing\n    * pricing enticements\n    * dynamic and variable pricing\n    * bundling\n- 8175-95 Explain the concept of bundling products and/or services.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * the definition of bundling —combining multiple products to be sold as one package\n    * the concept that there are more venue opportunities through bundling beyond the primary product or service (e.g., licensing, food and beverage)\n\nTERTIARY COMPETENCIES (never required; name any the work clearly shows under ALSO SHOWN)\n- 8175-40 Identify the components (the Ps) of the marketing mix as they relate to sports, entertainment, and event marketing.\n- 8175-66 Explain pricing and the factors that affect pricing decisions.\n- 8175-97 Explain on-site merchandising and concessions as they relate to sports, entertainment, and event facilities.\n- 8175-101 Explain competition as it relates to the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n\nTEACHER GRADING NOTES\nCheck the Break-Even Math section.\n\n- Formula: Did they show their work? (Fixed Costs / Profit per unit).\n- Strategy: Did they correctly identify \"Skimming\" vs. \"Penetration\"?\n\nPASSING RULE: APPROVED when Milestone 1 (the primary competency) is MET and Milestones 2, 3 and 4 are each MET or PARTIALLY MET. Tertiary competencies never affect the result.\n\nPASSING STANDARD\nThe work does every part of the task the assignment asks for, in the student's own words. Specific examples, not general statements. Judge each competency only by what this assignment asks the student to do. The lines under a competency are examples of what evidence can look like, not a checklist: one specific, well-done example that fits the task is enough, and a part of a competency the task does not ask for is never required."
