    "definitionOfDone": "ASSIGNMENT: L19 ALBUM RELEASE PROJECT (THE DROP)\nCOURSE: 8175 Sports Entertainment and Event Marketing\nCOMPETENCIES ASSESSED: 8175-93, 8175-73, 8175-104\n\nTHE TASK (what the student was asked to do)\nTitle: Project: The Album Drop (Product and Promotion)\n\nDescription:\n\nAfter months in the studio, your band’s album is finally ready to be released. Now you just have to make people care! You will be provided with a vinyl record for this assignment.\n\nDay 1 Checkpoint: The Product and The Brand\n\n- Alter the Sleeve: Design a cover that clearly displays the Artist Name and Album Name.\n- Alter the Vinyl: Modify the record itself.\n- Value Check: Every alteration should add \"perceived value.\"\n- Work Safely: Follow the class safety rules for cutting tools, hot glue and paint, and keep your station clear.\n\nDay 2 Checkpoint: The Hype Cycle\n\n- Timeline: Map out 2 months (1 month before release, 1 month after).\n- Schedule: Plan 7 unique promotional activities per week.\n- Required Events: 1 Public Appearance, 1 Fan Interaction, 1 Interview.\n\nDay 3 Checkpoint: The Pitch\n\n- Presentation: Create a pitch outlining your plan.\n- Include: Customer Profile, Branding Message, Ad Mockup, PR Talking Points, and Measurable Success Metrics.\n- Return on Investment: Estimate what your two-month plan would cost and how you would know it paid off (sales, streams or followers gained for each dollar spent).\n\nWhat your work needs to show:\n\n- I can describe the parts of a promotional campaign by mapping a two-month hype timeline and pitching a customer profile, branding message, ad mockup, PR talking points and success metrics (required)\n- I can explain how different kinds of promotion work together by mixing them into my weekly schedule, including a public appearance, a fan interaction and an interview\n- I can explain why a marketing plan's return on investment matters by estimating my two-month plan's cost and showing how sales, streams or followers per dollar prove it paid off\n\nYou can also show:\n\n- I can work safely by following the class rules for cutting tools, hot glue and paint while altering my sleeve and vinyl, and keeping my station clear\n- I can explain target marketing by building my customer profile around one specific group of fans and aiming my branding message and ad mockup at them\n- I can plan a product fans will value by altering my sleeve and vinyl so every change adds perceived value and the cover clearly shows the artist and album name\n- I can explain a product's life cycle by planning my timeline so promotion builds interest the month before release and keeps the album selling the month after\n- I can identify the parts of an advertisement by making an ad mockup that clearly shows the album, the artist and a reason for fans to listen or buy\n- I can explain what an ad is trying to do and how to measure it by tying my success metrics to my ad mockup\n- I can use public relations tools by writing PR talking points the band could use in the interview and public appearance to get positive attention for the album\n\nSubmission:\n\nSubmit your Presentation Slides here.\n\nPRIMARY COMPETENCY (Milestone 1; must be MET)\n- 8175-93 Describe the elements of sports, entertainment, and event promotional campaigns.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * event, product, or service\n    * campaign objectives\n    * target marketing\n    * campaign activities and schedule\n    * budget\n    * key performance indicators (KPI)\n\nSECONDARY COMPETENCIES (must be at least PARTIALLY MET)\n- 8175-73 Identify the roles of advertising, publicity, sales promotion, direct marketing, and personal selling in the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * advertising\n    * publicity\n    * sales promotion\n    * personal selling\n    * the advantages and disadvantages of each\n- 8175-104 Describe the importance of analyzing the marketing plan to determine ROI of the spend.\n    * Description should include the relationship between analyzing market research information and ROI.\n\nTERTIARY COMPETENCIES (never required; name any the work clearly shows under ALSO SHOWN)\n- 8175-22 Demonstrate workplace safety.\n- 8175-42 Explain the concept of target marketing.\n- 8175-60 Explain product and service planning in sports, entertainment, and events.\n- 8175-61 Explain product life cycle.\n- 8175-75 Describe the elements of an advertisement.\n- 8175-77 Identify the goals of advertising and the methods of evaluating an advertisement’s success.\n- 8175-84 Describe public relations (PR) tools.\n\nTEACHER GRADING NOTES\nUse the Checkpoints to pace the grading.\n\n- Day 1 (Product): Pass/Fail check. Did they alter the sleeve AND the record?\n- Day 2 (Timeline): Spot check the \"7 activities.\"\n- Day 3 (Presentation): Grade the \"Cohesive Branding.\"\n\nPASSING RULE: APPROVED when Milestone 1 (the primary competency) is MET and Milestones 2, 3 and 4 are each MET or PARTIALLY MET. Tertiary competencies never affect the result.\n\nPASSING STANDARD\nThe work does every part of the task the assignment asks for, in the student's own words. Specific examples, not general statements. Judge each competency only by what this assignment asks the student to do. The lines under a competency are examples of what evidence can look like, not a checklist: one specific, well-done example that fits the task is enough, and a part of a competency the task does not ask for is never required."
