    "definitionOfDone": "ASSIGNMENT: L20 MY CAUSE MY CLEATS (PARTNERSHIP ANALYSIS)\nCOURSE: 8175 Sports Entertainment and Event Marketing\nCOMPETENCIES ASSESSED: 8175-83, 8175-11, 8175-90\n\nTHE TASK (what the student was asked to do)\nTitle: Assignment: My Cause My Cleats (The Power of Strategic Partnership)\n\nDescription:\n\nWhen an athlete partners with a non profit, it is a powerful form of Co Branding. It builds their public image while driving massive awareness for a cause. We are looking at the NFL \"My Cause My Cleats\" campaign.\n\nThe Task:\n\nSelect one NFL athlete and the Non Profit organization they represented. Answer the following in a minimum of 100 words:\n\n- Who is the athlete and what is the organization?\n- The Why: Explain the connection. Did the athlete choose this cause because of a personal experience?\n- The Impact: From a marketing perspective, how does this partnership help the athlete's personal brand and the non profit organization?\n\nWhat your work needs to show:\n\n- I can explain public relations in sports by showing, as a marketer would, how the My Cause My Cleats partnership helps both the athlete's personal brand and the non profit (required)\n- I can see the bigger picture by connecting why my athlete chose this cause to what both the athlete and the non profit gain from it\n- I can identify sponsorship trends by explaining how my athlete and non profit teaming up for My Cause My Cleats is co-branding with a cause\n\nYou can also show:\n\n- I can think critically by backing up my answer about why the athlete chose this cause with facts I found, not just a guess\n- I can show respect for others by describing my athlete's cause and the people it serves with care\n\nSubmission:\n\nSubmit your analysis as a Text Entry.\n\nPRIMARY COMPETENCY (Milestone 1; must be MET)\n- 8175-83 Explain public relations as related to the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n    * Explanation should include the definition of public relations as it relates to the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n\nSECONDARY COMPETENCIES (must be at least PARTIALLY MET)\n- 8175-11 Demonstrate big-picture thinking.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * defining big-picture thinking as an understanding of one's role in fulfilling the mission of the workplace and a consideration of the social, economic, and environmental effects of one's actions\n    * identifying the organization’s structure, culture, policies, and procedures, as well as its role and position within the community, industry, and economy\n- 8175-90 Identify current trends in sponsorship within the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * examples of current sponsorships (e.g., companies sponsoring an athlete, team, or venue; digital channels that connect with fans)\n    * trends within these sponsorships\n\nTERTIARY COMPETENCIES (never required; name any the work clearly shows under ALSO SHOWN)\n- 8175-2 Demonstrate critical thinking and problem-solving.\n- 8175-8 Demonstrate respect for diversity.\n\nTEACHER GRADING NOTES\nScan student responses for the \"Why.\" If the student identifies the athlete, the charity, and provides a genuine reason for the partnership, award full points.\n\nPASSING RULE: APPROVED when Milestone 1 (the primary competency) is MET and Milestones 2, 3 and 4 are each MET or PARTIALLY MET. Tertiary competencies never affect the result.\n\nPASSING STANDARD\nThe work does every part of the task the assignment asks for, in the student's own words. Specific examples, not general statements. Judge each competency only by what this assignment asks the student to do. The lines under a competency are examples of what evidence can look like, not a checklist: one specific, well-done example that fits the task is enough, and a part of a competency the task does not ask for is never required."
