    "definitionOfDone": "ASSIGNMENT: L21 WHO OWNS IT? (LICENSING AND LAW)\nCOURSE: 8175 Sports Entertainment and Event Marketing\nCOMPETENCIES ASSESSED: 8175-87, 8175-88, 8175-58\n\nTHE TASK (what the student was asked to do)\nTitle: Assignment: The Naming Rights Bid and Ambush Marketing\n\nDescription:\n\nSports teams make massive amounts of money by selling the name of their stadium. This is called Naming Rights. Other brands try to cheat the system by advertising *near* the event without paying the fee. This is called Ambush Marketing.\n\nThe Task:\n\nComplete the two sections below.\n\nPart 1: The Naming Rights Bid\n\n- Scenario: Clover Hill High School is selling the naming rights to the football stadium.\n- Select a Brand: Choose a real company.\n- The Pitch: Write a 5 sentence proposal. Why does this brand want their name on our field?\n\nPart 2: Ambush Marketing Analysis\n\n- Define It: What is Ambush Marketing?\n- The Example: Brands finding ways to get on camera without paying the Olympic Committee.\n- The Verdict: Is Ambush Marketing genius or dishonest? Explain your answer in 2 sentences.\n- Sponsor or Endorser: Explain the difference between a sponsorship (a brand paying a team, venue or event) and an endorsement (a brand paying a person). Give one reason a brand might choose each.\n\nWhat your work needs to show:\n\n- I can explain how sponsorships work by writing a 5-sentence naming rights proposal for why my chosen real company wants its name on the Clover Hill football stadium (required)\n- I can compare sponsorships and endorsements by explaining the difference between them and giving one reason a brand might choose each\n- I can explain brand protection by defining ambush marketing and judging in 2 sentences whether it is genius or dishonest toward the brands that paid to sponsor the event\n\nYou can also show:\n\n- I can identify the rules that protect sponsors by explaining what fee or rule ambush marketers get around when they advertise near the Olympics without paying the Olympic Committee\n- I can explain how stakeholders are connected by showing what the brand, the school and its fans would each gain from the naming rights deal\n\nSubmission:\n\nSubmit your Bid and Analysis as a Text Entry.\n\nPRIMARY COMPETENCY (Milestone 1; must be MET)\n- 8175-87 Explain sponsorship and endorsements as they relate to the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * the definition of sponsorship\n    * the definition of endorsement\n    * the differences between sponsorship and endorsement\n    * types of sponsorship (e.g., naming rights, signage, exclusivity)\n\nSECONDARY COMPETENCIES (must be at least PARTIALLY MET)\n- 8175-88 Compare the reasons for using sponsorships vs. endorsements.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * increasing sales\n    * introducing new products/services\n    * enhancing company image\n    * offering a nontraditional avenue to reach live audiences\n    * offsetting costs\n    * endorsement advantages (e.g., consumer identification with famous athletes or entertainers and winning teams)\n    * disadvantages (e.g., controversial celebrities or negative publicity, expense, potential for consumers to doubt the sincerity of the endorser)\n- 8175-58 Explain product licensing and brand protection.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * licensing\n    * royalties\n    * licensor\n    * licensee\n    * intellectual property (IP)\n    * broadcast flags\n    * piracy\n\nTERTIARY COMPETENCIES (never required; name any the work clearly shows under ALSO SHOWN)\n- 8175-89 Identify legal restrictions placed on endorsements and sponsorships.\n- 8175-91 Describe the components and relationships between stakeholders.\n\nTEACHER GRADING NOTES\nCheck for the definition of Ambush Marketing.\n\n- Correct: A brand trying to look like a sponsor without paying for it.\n- The Bid: In the stadium activity, award creativity points for the \"activation\" ideas.\n\nPASSING RULE: APPROVED when Milestone 1 (the primary competency) is MET and Milestones 2, 3 and 4 are each MET or PARTIALLY MET. Tertiary competencies never affect the result.\n\nPASSING STANDARD\nThe work does every part of the task the assignment asks for, in the student's own words. Specific examples, not general statements. Judge each competency only by what this assignment asks the student to do. The lines under a competency are examples of what evidence can look like, not a checklist: one specific, well-done example that fits the task is enough, and a part of a competency the task does not ask for is never required."
