    "definitionOfDone": "ASSIGNMENT: L23 INDUSTRY PUBLICATION PROJECT (THE ZINE)\nCOURSE: 8175 Sports Entertainment and Event Marketing\nCOMPETENCIES ASSESSED: 8175-1, 8175-75, 8175-82\n\nTHE TASK (what the student was asked to do)\nTitle: Project: The Industry Publication (Create Your Own Magazine)\n\nDescription:\n\nOver the next several class periods, you will produce a professional publication (Magazine/Zine) that focuses on an industry of your choosing.\n\nThe Requirements:\n\n- Cover: Attractive, eye-catching front cover.\n- Articles: One article per group member (Must include one Opinion Piece and one News Piece).\n- Advertisements: One colorful, eye-catching advertisement per group member.\n- Targeting: All content must be relevant to your demographic.\n- Going Digital: In your presentation, explain two ways your magazine could make money online (digital ads, subscriptions, sponsored posts or video).\n\nThe Schedule (Checkpoints):\n\n- Day 1 (The Plan): Form groups. Assign roles. Submit Topic and Target Market.\n- Day 2 (The Draft): Write articles. Complete a Peer Review.\n- Day 3 (The Ads): Create advertisement pieces.\n- Day 4 (The Layout): Compile the pieces.\n- Day 5 (The Presentation): Bring your finished, bound project to class. Give a group presentation highlighting your demographic, articles, and pricing strategy.\n\nWhat your work needs to show:\n\n- I can create an original publication by designing an eye-catching front cover and laying out our articles and ads so the magazine looks finished (required)\n- I can design an effective advertisement by making a colorful, eye-catching ad that clearly shows what is being sold and is aimed at our demographic\n- I can explain how digital media makes money by describing two ways our magazine could earn online, such as digital ads, subscriptions, sponsored posts or video\n\nYou can also show:\n\n- I can work well on a team by doing my assigned role, writing my own article and giving useful feedback in the Day 2 Peer Review\n- I can manage my time by meeting each daily checkpoint, from our Topic and Target Market on Day 1 to the finished, bound project on Day 5\n- I can present to an audience by helping give a group presentation that clearly highlights our demographic, articles and pricing strategy\n- I can compare traditional and digital marketing by explaining how making money online for our magazine differs from selling the printed, bound copy\n\nSubmission:\n\nSubmit a digital PDF of your publication here, and hand in the physical copy.\n\nPRIMARY COMPETENCY (Milestone 1; must be MET)\n- 8175-1 Demonstrate creativity and innovation.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * discussing the importance of creativity and innovation in the workplace\n    * brainstorming and contributing ideas, strategies, and solutions\n    * developing and/or improving products, services, or processes\n    * identifying and allocating available resources\n\nSECONDARY COMPETENCIES (must be at least PARTIALLY MET)\n- 8175-75 Describe the elements of an advertisement.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * inducement (who, what, where, when, and why)\n    * content\n    * call to action\n- 8175-82 Explain how digital media can be used to generate revenue.\n    * Explanation should include examples of available platforms and the target market of each.\nExplanation should also include a discussion of how user engagement in a platform influences revenue generation. \n.\n\nTERTIARY COMPETENCIES (never required; name any the work clearly shows under ALSO SHOWN)\n- 8175-10 Collaborate with team members.\n- 8175-14 Manage time and resources.\n- 8175-50 Develop presentation skills as a form of business communication.\n- 8175-81 Compare traditional marketing and digital media marketing.\n\nTEACHER GRADING NOTES\nUse the Checkpoints to reduce your grading load. Grade Day 1 and 2 for completion. Grade the final product on \"Professionalism\" of binding/layout.\n\nPASSING RULE: APPROVED when Milestone 1 (the primary competency) is MET and Milestones 2, 3 and 4 are each MET or PARTIALLY MET. Tertiary competencies never affect the result.\n\nPASSING STANDARD\nThe work does every part of the task the assignment asks for, in the student's own words. Specific examples, not general statements. Judge each competency only by what this assignment asks the student to do. The lines under a competency are examples of what evidence can look like, not a checklist: one specific, well-done example that fits the task is enough, and a part of a competency the task does not ask for is never required."
