    "definitionOfDone": "ASSIGNMENT: L26 PREPARING YOUR ELEVATOR PITCH\nCOURSE: 8175 Sports Entertainment and Event Marketing\nCOMPETENCIES ASSESSED: 8175-12, 8175-109\n\nTHE TASK (what the student was asked to do)\nTitle: Assignment: Preparing Your Elevator Pitch (The 30 Second Commercial)\n\nDescription:\n\nYou only get one chance to make a great first impression! An \"Elevator Pitch\" is a quick summary of your background and experience.\n\nThe Task:\n\nFill in at least two to three specific examples for each category below.\n\n- Education: Courses that inspired you.\n- Top Strengths: Specific skills.\n- Achievements: Awards, certifications, personal bests.\n- Academic Projects: Research projects or events you planned.\n- Target Organizations: What type of companies interest you?\n- Inspiration: Who do you look up to?\n- Interesting Facts: What sets you apart from the typical student?\n\nWhat your work needs to show:\n\n- I can describe what I bring to a career by filling in two to three specific examples from my own life for Education, Top Strengths, Achievements and Academic Projects (required)\n- I can start a networking plan by naming two to three types of companies that interest me and two to three people I look up to\n\nYou can also show:\n\n- I can prepare to speak about myself by choosing Interesting Facts that set me apart and would sound strong said out loud in a 30-second pitch\n\nSubmission:\n\nSubmit your completed document as a File Upload.\n\nPRIMARY COMPETENCY (Milestone 1; must be MET)\n- 8175-12 Demonstrate career- and life-management skills.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * recognizing the importance of education and career planning (e.g., minimum job qualifications, advancement and professional-development opportunities)\n    * identifying available benefits and professional resources (e.g., labor unions, professional organizations, employee-assistance programs, insurance and retirement benefits)\n    * managing personal growth and wellness (e.g., stress management, self-care, financial planning)\n    * setting goals (e.g., specific, measurable, attainable, realistic, time-bound [SMART] goals)\n\nSECONDARY COMPETENCIES (must be at least PARTIALLY MET)\n- 8175-109 Create a networking plan in the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * explaining the importance of networking\n    * researching contacts\n    * identifying effective networking practices and tools\n    * determining when and where networking should be done (e.g., conferences, events, social media)\n    * adding value when making networking connections\n    * maintaining relationships over time\n\nTERTIARY COMPETENCIES (never required; name any the work clearly shows under ALSO SHOWN)\n- 8175-7 Demonstrate listening and speaking skills.\n\nTEACHER GRADING NOTES\nCheck for specificity. In the \"Strengths\" and \"Achievements\" boxes, look for specific examples (e.g., \"Led a team of 5 students\") rather than vague adjectives.\n\nPASSING RULE: APPROVED when Milestone 1 (the primary competency) is MET and Milestones 2, 3 and 4 are each MET or PARTIALLY MET. Tertiary competencies never affect the result.\n\nPASSING STANDARD\nThe work does every part of the task the assignment asks for, in the student's own words. Specific examples, not general statements. Judge each competency only by what this assignment asks the student to do. The lines under a competency are examples of what evidence can look like, not a checklist: one specific, well-done example that fits the task is enough, and a part of a competency the task does not ask for is never required."
