    "definitionOfDone": "ASSIGNMENT: L28 MARKET RESEARCH (SURVEY DESIGN)\nCOURSE: 8175 Sports Entertainment and Event Marketing\nCOMPETENCIES ASSESSED: 8175-62, 8175-2, 8175-17\n\nTHE TASK (what the student was asked to do)\nTitle: Assignment: Designing Unbiased Surveys (Google Forms)\n\nDescription:\n\nWe are in the process of designing a new service. To do this successfully, we need to know what the customer wants. However, asking the wrong questions leads to wrong answers.\n\nThe Task:\n\n- Create: Open Google Forms and start a new blank form.\n- Design Questions: Create three survey questions.\n\n- Requirement A: Include two quantitative questions (Multiple Choice or Scale).\n- Requirement B: Include one qualitative question (Open-ended paragraph).\n\n- Bias Check: Review your questions. Do they suggest the \"correct\" answer? If so, rewrite them to be neutral.\n\nWhat your work needs to show:\n\n- I can explain what market research is for by writing two quantitative questions and one qualitative question that would find out what customers want from the new service (required)\n- I can spot and fix bias by checking each question and explaining in one or two sentences which question I rewrote and what I changed to make it neutral\n- I can use Google Forms by building my three questions, with answer choices for the two quantitative ones, and pasting the form's send link\n\nYou can also show:\n\n- I can explain why research matters to a marketing plan by asking questions whose answers could actually help us decide how to design the new service\n\nSubmission:\n\nClick the \"Send\" button on your form, copy the link, and paste the URL as your submission.\n\nBelow the link, type your three questions exactly as they appear on the form, with the answer choices for the two quantitative questions. Then, in one or two sentences, say which question you rewrote after the Bias Check and what you changed.\n\nPRIMARY COMPETENCY (Milestone 1; must be MET)\n- 8175-62 Describe the role of market research.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * define primary research and secondary research\n    * emphasize that market research involves obtaining necessary information to make sound product-development decisions (e.g., data to ensure that products are relevant to target markets)\n\nSECONDARY COMPETENCIES (must be at least PARTIALLY MET)\n- 8175-2 Demonstrate critical thinking and problem-solving.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * recognizing and analyzing problems\n    * evaluating potential solutions and resources\n    * using a logical approach to make decisions and solve problems\n    * implementing effective courses of action\n- 8175-17 Maintain working knowledge of current information-technology (IT) systems.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * hardware and devices (e.g., peripherals)\n    * software and applications\n    * cloud-based services\n    * file-sharing techniques\n    * emerging technologies\n    * troubleshooting protocols and techniques\n\nTERTIARY COMPETENCIES (never required; name any the work clearly shows under ALSO SHOWN)\n- 8175-103 Describe the importance of research in developing a marketing plan.\n\nTEACHER GRADING NOTES\nCheck for the question types immediately.\n\n- Quantitative (2): Are there multiple choice or scale questions?\n- Qualitative (1): Is there an open-ended text box question?\n- Bias Check: Read one question aloud. If it leads the witness, deduct points.\n\nPASSING RULE: APPROVED when Milestone 1 (the primary competency) is MET and Milestones 2, 3 and 4 are each MET or PARTIALLY MET. Tertiary competencies never affect the result.\n\nPASSING STANDARD\nThe work does every part of the task the assignment asks for, in the student's own words. Specific examples, not general statements. Judge each competency only by what this assignment asks the student to do. The lines under a competency are examples of what evidence can look like, not a checklist: one specific, well-done example that fits the task is enough, and a part of a competency the task does not ask for is never required."
