    "definitionOfDone": "ASSIGNMENT: L33 THE PAPER TRAIL (RESUMES AND APPLICATIONS)\nCOURSE: 8175 Sports Entertainment and Event Marketing\nCOMPETENCIES ASSESSED: 8175-110, 8175-108\n\nTHE TASK (what the student was asked to do)\nTitle: Assignment: The 6-Second Resume\n\nDescription:\n\nRecruiters spend an average of 6 seconds looking at a resume before deciding to keep it or trash it. Your resume must be clean, professional, and easy to read.\n\nThe Task:\n\nCreate a one page professional resume.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Header: Name, Professional Email, Phone Number, LinkedIn URL.\n- Education: School, Graduation Date, GPA.\n- Experience: List any jobs, volunteer work, or club leadership.\n- Skills: List Soft Skills and Hard Skills.\n- The \"Action Verb\" Rule: Every bullet point under your experience must start with an Action Verb.\n- The Target: Find one real job or internship posting on TeamWork Online, Indeed, Handshake or a team's careers page that your resume could be sent to, and paste the link at the bottom.\n\nWhat your work needs to show:\n\n- I can write a professional resume by making a one-page resume with the full header, education, experience bullets that each start with an action verb, and soft and hard skills (required)\n- I can find job search resources by locating one real job or internship posting on TeamWork Online, Indeed, Handshake or a team's careers page and pasting its link\n\nYou can also show:\n\n- I can stay safe online by using a professional email address and sharing only the contact details my resume header asks for\n- I can complete a job application by filling in my school, graduation date, GPA and experience accurately, the same way I would on a real application\n\nSubmission:\n\nSubmit your Resume as a PDF file.\n\nPRIMARY COMPETENCY (Milestone 1; must be MET)\n- 8175-110 Develop a résumé and cover letter.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * a résumé, including\n    * personal information\n    * educational background\n    * work experience\n    * AI-related skills\n    * extracurricular activities and interests\n    * references\n    * an up-to-date professional profile on job networking sites (e.g., LinkedIn)\n    * a cover letter providing information related to the specific job and following a standard professional format\n\nSECONDARY COMPETENCIES (must be at least PARTIALLY MET)\n- 8175-108 Identify resources for a job search in the sports, entertainment, and event industries.\n    Evidence can include (examples, not all required):\n    * career search websites\n    * job fairs\n    * job placement agencies and recruiters\n    * social media sites (e.g., LinkedIn)\n    * association memberships\n    * professional presentation of oneself on social media\n\nTERTIARY COMPETENCIES (never required; name any the work clearly shows under ALSO SHOWN)\n- 8175-34 Identify Internet safety issues and procedures for complying with acceptable use standards.\n- 8175-111 Complete a job application.\n\nTEACHER GRADING NOTES\nGrade the Formatting. Zero tolerance for typos. Ensure consistency in dates and fonts.\n\nPASSING RULE: APPROVED when Milestone 1 (the primary competency) is MET and Milestones 2, 3 and 4 are each MET or PARTIALLY MET. Tertiary competencies never affect the result.\n\nPASSING STANDARD\nThe work does every part of the task the assignment asks for, in the student's own words. Specific examples, not general statements. Judge each competency only by what this assignment asks the student to do. The lines under a competency are examples of what evidence can look like, not a checklist: one specific, well-done example that fits the task is enough, and a part of a competency the task does not ask for is never required."
