    "promptText": "Assignment: The Cavalier Shop Survey\n\nBackground:\n\nA business that guesses what customers want wastes money on products nobody buys. Market research means collecting information from real customers before making decisions. A survey is one of the simplest ways to do it.\n\nYou can't market to everyone. A store that tries to appeal to everyone ends up appealing to no one. Freshmen who want spirit wear, parents with the credit cards and teachers buying a staff shirt all want different things and hear about events in different places. Picking a target market means deciding which group you're trying to reach.\n\nWhere data comes from:\n\n- Primary data is information you collect yourself, for your own question: surveys, interviews, focus groups, or watching how customers move through a store.\n- Secondary data is information someone else already collected: sales records, social media analytics, industry reports, news articles, and government data like the U.S. Census.\n- In sports and entertainment, common sources include ticketing and point-of-sale (POS) records (who bought what, and when), TV and streaming ratings, social media and website analytics, and reports from leagues and trade publications.\n- Secondary data is usually faster and cheaper, so researchers check it first. But it was collected for someone else's question, so it often can't tell you exactly what you need. That's when you collect primary data.\n\nFor the Cavalier Shop, the store's own POS sales records show which items sell and when, CHHS enrollment numbers show how many possible customers there are, and the store's Instagram insights show which posts people engage with. Only a survey can ask people why.\n\nTwo kinds of data from a survey:\n\n- Quantitative data is numbers you can count and compare: multiple choice, checkboxes, rating scales. \"62% of students hear about events on Instagram\" is quantitative.\n- Qualitative data is ideas and opinions in people's own words: short-answer and paragraph questions. It's harder to count but can surprise you with ideas you never thought of.\n\nBias is the enemy of good research. A biased question pushes people toward an answer, so the results tell you what you wanted to hear instead of the truth. Watch for these:\n\n- Leading words: \"amazing,\" \"great,\" \"awful\" tell people how to feel.\n- Leading questions: \"Don't you agree…?\" makes it awkward to say no.\n- Loaded choices: when one option is described as clearly better (\"cheap\" vs. \"quality\"), people pick the \"right\" one.\n- Too-narrow options: a drop-down list can only collect the answers you already thought of. That's why Question 3 is open-ended.\n\nRespect people's privacy. Turning off email collection lets people answer honestly without worrying about who will see their name.\n\nKey Terms:\n\n- Market research: collecting information about customers to make better business decisions.\n- Target market: the specific group of customers a business is trying to reach.\n- Survey: a set of questions asked to many people to collect information.\n- Quantitative data: information in numbers that can be counted and compared.\n- Qualitative data: information in words, like opinions and ideas.\n- Bias: anything in a question that pushes people toward a certain answer.\n- Leading question: a question worded to suggest the \"right\" answer.\n- Primary data: information you collect yourself for your own research question.\n- Secondary data: information that someone else already collected, like sales records or published reports.\n\nExample:\n\nFixing a biased question, step by step.\n\n- Biased: \"How great was the new snack selection at our last home game?\"\n- What's wrong: \"great\" tells people how to feel, and it assumes they liked the selection.\n- Neutral: \"How would you rate the snack selection at the last home game?\" (scale of 1 = Very poor to 5 = Excellent)\n- Why it's better: someone who disliked the snacks can say so just as easily as someone who liked them, so the store gets honest data.\n\nYour Task:\n\nYou are a market researcher for the Cavalier Shop. Your job is to build a short survey that finds out how to reach our customers and what they want, without pushing them toward an answer.\n\nPart 1: The Target Market. Who should take this survey: students, parents or staff? Pick one group and explain why they matter most to the store.\n\nPart 2: Build the Survey. In Google Forms, build a survey with these three questions, set up exactly as shown:\n\n- Question 1 (checkboxes, select up to two): How do you most frequently receive news and updates regarding Clover Hill High School events? Options: Canvas Announcements, CHHS Instagram, ParentSquare, Morning Announcements, Word of Mouth/Friends.\n- Question 2 (linear scale, 1 = Not at all important, 5 = Extremely important): On a scale of 1 to 5, how important is it to you that the profits from your merchandise purchases directly fund CHHS student activities?\n- Question 3 (short answer): What is one specific type of product or service you would like to see offered at The Cavalier Shop that is currently unavailable?\n\nTurn off email collection in the form's settings. Paste your form's link into this document.\n\nPart 3: Why These Questions Work. For each question, say whether it collects quantitative or qualitative data, and explain what the store will learn from it.\n\nPart 4: Spot the Bias. Each question below is biased. For each one, explain what's wrong with it, then rewrite it so it's neutral.\n\n- \"How much do you love our amazing new t-shirts?\"\n- \"Don't you agree the store should stay open after school?\"\n- \"Would you rather buy a cheap hoodie online or a quality Cavalier hoodie that supports your school?\"\n\nPart 5: Beyond the Survey. Our survey is primary data. Name two more sources of data the Cavalier Shop could use: one more primary source and one secondary source. For each, say what it would tell the store that the survey can't.\n\nUse these terms accurately: market research, target market, survey, quantitative data, qualitative data, bias, leading question, primary data, secondary data.\n\nWhat your work needs to show:\n\n- I can build a market research survey by making the Google Form, pasting its link, and explaining whether each question collects quantitative or qualitative data and what the store learns (required)\n- I can spot and fix bias by explaining what’s wrong with each of the three biased questions and rewriting each one so it’s neutral\n- I can identify sources of market research data by choosing one more primary and one secondary source for the Cavalier Shop and saying what each adds that the survey can’t\n\nYou can also show:\n\n- I can use Google Forms like a researcher by setting up checkboxes for up to two, a 1-to-5 linear scale and a short answer, with email collection turned off\n- I can choose a target market by picking students, parents or staff and explaining why that group matters most to the Cavalier Shop\n- I can explain what motivates customers by saying what Question 2 will tell the store about whether supporting CHHS student activities makes people want to buy",
