    "promptText": "Assignment: The Data Analysis Report\n\nBackground:\n\nNumbers don't speak for themselves. \"70% of students chose Instagram\" is a fact. What it means for the store is the part a manager has to figure out: maybe the store should post on Instagram before every game, and stop spending time on flyers nobody reads.\n\nFrom seeing a number to making a decision:\n\n- What does it say? State the finding with its number.\n- What does it mean? Explain why that matters to the store.\n- What should we do? Turn it into an action.\n\nReading the two kinds of results:\n\n- Quantitative results (Questions 1 and 2) come as percentages and charts. Look for the biggest and smallest numbers and anything surprising.\n- Qualitative results (Question 3) are people's own words. Look for themes: the same idea showing up again and again (\"hoodies,\" \"snacks,\" \"more sizes\"). Count how many responses mention each theme so you can report it as a number.\n\nCite the data. A manager's recommendation is only as strong as its evidence.\n\n- Weak: \"A lot of people want hoodies.\"\n- Strong: \"According to the survey, 31% of written suggestions asked for hoodies, more than any other item.\"\n\nEvery part of marketing needs research. Businesses (and DECA) describe marketing as seven functions, and each one makes better decisions with data:\n\n- Product/service management: what to sell. (Question 3 asked what customers want.)\n- Pricing: what to charge. (Our survey didn't ask this; a future one could.)\n- Promotion: how to reach customers. (Question 1 asked where people get school news.)\n- Channel management (place): where and how customers buy, like in the store, at games or online.\n- Selling: what to say to a customer. (Question 2 shows whether \"it funds student activities\" is a strong selling point.)\n- Market planning: who to target and what the overall plan is.\n- Marketing-information management: collecting and organizing the data itself, which is what this whole project is.\n\nA store that guesses on even one of these, like pricing, can lose money even when everything else is right.\n\nAn executive summary is a short report written for a busy decision-maker. It gets to the point fast: what we found, what it means, and what we recommend. Use complete sentences, a professional tone and no slang.\n\nKey Terms:\n\n- Data point: one specific finding from the results, usually a number.\n- Trend: a pattern that shows up across the data.\n- Quantitative data: results in numbers, like percentages and ratings.\n- Qualitative data: results in words, like written suggestions.\n- Marketing functions: the parts of marketing a business manages, including product, pricing, promotion, channel management (place) and selling.\n- Executive summary: a short, professional report that gives a decision-maker the key findings and a recommendation.\n- Recommendation: a specific action the business should take, backed by evidence.\n\nExample:\n\nOne data point and recommendation, with invented numbers (yours must use the real results).\n\n\"According to the survey, 64% of students said they get school news from the CHHS Instagram, but only 9% from Morning Announcements. The store currently promotes sales mostly through announcements, which means most of our customers may never hear about them. I recommend that the Cavalier Shop post every sale and new product on the CHHS Instagram the morning it starts, and move the time we spend writing announcements into making those posts.\"\n\nYour Task:\n\nOur survey is done. Now you're the manager. Use the class survey results your teacher shares to write a report that tells the Cavalier Shop what the data says and what it should do about it.\n\nPart 1: Three Data Points. Pick three interesting findings from the results. You can mix numbers (from Questions 1 and 2) and ideas people wrote in (from Question 3). For each one, write the finding with its number (\"42% of students said…\") and explain what it means for the store.\n\nPart 2: One Change. Recommend one specific change to how the Cavalier Shop is set up or run (its layout, hours, products or promotion).\n\nPart 3: Back It Up. Explain how one or more of your three data points supports your recommendation. Use the numbers.\n\nPart 4: One Survey, Every Function. Marketing has several functions. For three of them (choose from product, pricing, promotion, place/distribution and selling), write one sentence on how our survey results help that part of the store, or one question a future survey should ask to help it.\n\nWrite it as one professional report of at least 250 words, the kind you could hand to the principal. Use only the real survey results: making up or changing numbers is an academic integrity violation.\n\nUse these terms accurately: data point, trend, quantitative data, qualitative data, executive summary, recommendation, marketing functions.\n\nWhat your work needs to show:\n\n- I can evaluate survey results by stating three findings from the real data, each with its number, and explaining what each one means for the Cavalier Shop (required)\n- I can solve a business problem with data by recommending one specific change to the Cavalier Shop’s layout, hours, products or promotion and backing it with numbers from my findings\n- I can explain why marketing functions need research by writing one sentence for each of three functions on how the survey helps it or what a future survey should ask\n\nYou can also show:\n\n- I can write a professional report of at least 250 words, in complete sentences with no slang, that I could hand to the principal\n- I can explain how a change affects store operations by showing how my recommended layout or hours change would change how the Cavalier Shop runs day to day\n- I can explain the value of market research by showing how the survey results help the Cavalier Shop make better marketing decisions than guessing",
