    "promptText": "Assignment: The Game Day Takeover\n\nBackground:\n\nEvery event has three parts: before, during and after. Fans only see the middle. Event managers are judged on all three.\n\nBefore: the plan.\n\n- A run of show is the event's schedule, minute by minute: when setup starts, when doors open, the busy times, when sales stop, and when cleanup is done.\n- A staffing chart lists every job, who does it, and who covers it if that person is out. On a busy night, every role needs a backup.\n- Goals are set before the event, not after, so you can honestly judge how it went.\n\nDuring: running it.\n\n- Follow the run of show, but expect surprises. A good manager solves the problem and writes down what happened.\n- Customer service still counts at a fast-moving table: greet people, keep the line moving, and thank every customer.\n- Closeout follows the Employee Handbook: count the money, run the Z-out report, count the inventory that's left, and pack up.\n\nAfter: the follow-up.\n\n- Recognize people. Events depend on people the crowd never sees: the athletic director who approved it, custodians who set up and cleaned, teachers and parents who helped, and the customers who showed up.\n- Thank them within a few days. A good thank-you is specific (\"Thank you for setting up our table before the gates opened\"), sincere and short.\n- Recognition can be private, like a handwritten note, or public, like a social post or a shout-out on the morning announcements.\n- People who feel appreciated say yes again. A thank-you is how you get help for the next event.\n- Evaluate it. Compare the results with your goals, then decide what to keep and what to change.\n\nPublic posts show the shop and the products. Don't post photos of students without a media release on file, and don't name customers without their permission.\n\nKey Terms:\n\n- Event manager: the person responsible for planning, running and following up on an event.\n- Run of show: a minute-by-minute schedule for an event.\n- Staffing chart: a list of every job at an event, who does it, and who backs them up.\n- Closeout: the steps that end a shift or event, like counting money and inventory.\n- Follow-up: what an event team does after the event, including thanking people and evaluating results.\n- Recognition: publicly or privately showing appreciation for someone's work.\n- Goal: a specific target set before the event, used to judge its success.\n\nExample:\n\nA sample handwritten thank-you note.\n\n\"Dear Mr. Lewis, thank you for setting up our tables and power cord before the gates opened on Friday. Because everything was ready early, we started selling during warm-ups and sold 40 items before kickoff. We couldn't have done it without you. Sincerely, the Cavalier Shop team\"\n\nYour Task:\n\nThe Cavalier Shop is taking over a home game. Our class will run a pop-up shop at the game, selling the products we made in class, from setup to cleanup. Then we'll do the part most first-time event managers forget: thank the people who made it happen and find out whether it worked.\n\nThis assignment takes two days with the event in between. Day 1: Parts 1–2. After the event: Parts 3–5.\n\nPart 1: The Run of Show. Build the pop-up's timeline, from setup to cleanup, using the game schedule your teacher shares. Mark the busy times (before the game and at halftime). Then write your role and your shift.\n\nPart 2: The Manager's Checklist. Complete:\n\n- a staffing chart: every role (shift lead, cashier, sales floor/runner, inventory and setup), who fills it, and who backs them up\n- a supply list: inventory count, starting cash and card reader, signs, table and anything else the shop needs\n- the event's goals: items sold, revenue, number of customers, and the longest wait you'd accept\n- one risk and your backup plan (for example: rain, a long line, or a card reader that won't connect)\n\nPart 3: Say Thank You. After the event:\n\n- List everyone who helped make the event happen, and what each person or group did.\n- Write a handwritten thank-you note to one staff member on your list. Be specific about what they did and why it mattered. Type the note's words into this document before you deliver it.\n- Draft a public thank-you to our customers, for social media or the morning announcements.\n\nPart 4: The Results. Compare what happened with the goals from Part 2. Use the real numbers from the event log. Then describe your own shift: what you did, one customer you served, and one problem that came up and how it was handled.\n\nPart 5: Keep and Change. Name one thing to keep and one thing to change for the next event, and explain each using the results.\n\nUse these terms accurately: event manager, run of show, staffing chart, closeout, follow-up, recognition, goal.\n\nWhat your work needs to show:\n\n- I can recognize the people behind an event by listing everyone who helped and what each did, writing one staff member a specific thank-you note, and drafting a customer thank-you (required)\n- I can plan staffing for an event by building a staffing chart showing every pop-up role, who fills it and who backs them up\n\nYou can also show:\n\n- I can work as part of a team by writing my role and shift in the run of show and describing how I helped the team during my shift\n- I can serve customers well by describing one customer I served during my shift and one problem that came up and how it was handled\n- I can manage an event by building a setup-to-cleanup run of show with the busy times marked, a supply list, and one risk with a backup plan\n- I can judge an event’s success by comparing its real numbers with our goals and naming one thing to keep and one to change, using the results",
