    "promptText": "Assignment: The Millionaire's Strike\n\nBackground:\n\nWhen pro athletes go on strike, it can look like millionaires arguing with billionaires. But a work stoppage hurts a lot of people who aren't rich at all, and it shows how labor and business work in every industry.\n\nAthletes have unions too. Every major U.S. pro league has a players' union, like the NFL Players Association. A union is a group of workers who bargain with their employer together instead of one at a time. Athletes negotiate minimum salaries, health care and safety rules the same way teachers, plumbers and auto workers do.\n\nThe contract and the stoppage:\n\n- Collective bargaining agreement (CBA): the contract between the owners and the players' union, usually for several years at a time.\n- Strike: the players refuse to play until they get a better deal.\n- Lockout: the owners refuse to let the players play until the players accept their terms.\n\nLeverage is the power one side has in a negotiation. Players have leverage because of specialization. An elite quarterback or point guard has skills almost no one else in the world has. If owners replaced their stars with amateurs, the quality would drop and fans would stop paying. Players also have options: during the 2011 NBA lockout, some players signed with teams in Europe and Asia. Owners can't move their stadiums overseas.\n\nThe ripple effect: during a strike, the players and owners can usually afford to wait. The people who lose their paychecks are the ushers, ticket takers, concession workers, parking attendants and security staff, plus the restaurants, bars and hotels near the stadium that count on game-day crowds. When many people lose income at once, they spend less, and that spreads to other businesses. That's how a sports dispute turns into local unemployment and a slowdown for a whole city.\n\nThe law behind the bargaining table. Two federal laws shape every sports labor fight:\n\n- Labor law: the National Labor Relations Act (1935) gives workers, including pro athletes, the right to form a union and bargain as a group. An employer can't refuse to negotiate with its workers' union.\n- Antitrust law: the Sherman Antitrust Act (1890) bans businesses from teaming up to limit competition. When all the owners in a league agree to a draft (players can't pick their team) or a salary cap (a limit on what teams can pay), that would normally look like competitors teaming up to hold down pay.\n- The trade-off: rules that owners and the players' union agree to in a CBA are protected from antitrust lawsuits. So owners actually need a CBA. In the 2011 NFL and NBA lockouts, the players' unions disbanded so that players could sue their leagues under antitrust law, which put pressure on owners to make a deal.\n- Baseball is the exception: a 1922 Supreme Court decision exempted MLB from antitrust law. In 1998, Congress passed the Curt Flood Act, which removed that exemption for issues about major league players' employment.\n\nHow to negotiate professionally: listen to the other side, look for trades (\"we'll accept 52/48 if you drop the extra games\"), and never insult the other side. In a real negotiation, a deal both sides can live with is better than no season at all.\n\nKey Terms:\n\n- Union (organized labor): a group of workers who negotiate with their employer together.\n- Collective bargaining agreement (CBA): the contract between a union and an employer.\n- Strike: when workers refuse to work to pressure the employer.\n- Lockout: when an employer refuses to let workers work to pressure the union.\n- Leverage: the power one side has to get what it wants in a negotiation.\n- Specialization: when workers have rare, specific skills that are hard to replace.\n- Unemployment: when people who want to work can't find a job or lose their income.\n- Productivity: how much value a worker produces compared with what they cost.\n- Labor law: laws that protect workers' right to form unions and bargain with employers, like the National Labor Relations Act.\n- Antitrust law: laws that stop businesses from teaming up to limit competition, like the Sherman Antitrust Act.\n\nExample:\n\nOne entry from a sample Collateral Damage Report.\n\n\"A concession worker at the ballpark: many concession workers are paid by the hour and work only on game nights. With about 70 home games in a season, a canceled season would wipe out that income completely. A worker who depends on that money to pay for a car or help with rent would have to find another job fast, and if many workers are looking at once, there may not be enough jobs to go around.\"\n\nYour Task:\n\nThe league's contract with its players has expired, and the season is about to be canceled. Your class will split into the Owners and the Players Association and negotiate a new contract, called a collective bargaining agreement (CBA), on four issues:\n\n- Revenue sharing: owners want 55% of TV money to the owners and 45% to the players. Players want 50/50.\n- Season length: owners want 2 more games. Players refuse, citing injury risk.\n- Minimum salary: players want rookies to earn at least $1,000,000. Owners want $750,000.\n- Overseas play: owners want to ban players from playing in other countries' leagues in the off-season, to protect against injury. Players want the right to play anywhere.\n\nIf the two sides can't agree before the end of class, the season is canceled.\n\nPart 1: Your Side. Say which side you're on. For each of the four issues, write what your side wants and one reason it's fair.\n\nPart 2: The Deal. During the negotiation, keep notes on one issue of your choice: each offer and counteroffer, in order, and which side made it. Afterward, record the final agreement on each issue, and explain what each side gave up to get there.\n\nPart 3: The Collateral Damage Report. If a Richmond team (like the Flying Squirrels or the Kickers) stopped playing for a season, who would get hurt? Name three specific local businesses or hourly jobs, and for each one explain exactly how its income depends on game days.\n\nPart 4: Who Has the Leverage? Explain why owners can't just fire striking players and hire replacements, and why players who can play overseas have extra bargaining power.\n\nPart 5: The Law. In a short paragraph, explain what right the National Labor Relations Act gives the players, and why the owners would rather have the draft and salary cap written into a CBA than just agree on them among themselves.\n\nUse these terms accurately: union, collective bargaining agreement, strike, lockout, leverage, specialization, unemployment, labor law, antitrust law.\n\nWhat your work needs to show:\n\n- I can analyze a labor dispute by giving my side's position and reason on all four issues, what each side gave up, and why players are hard to replace (required)\n- I can explain how labor law shapes pro sports by naming the right the National Labor Relations Act gives players and why owners want the draft and salary cap bargained\n- I can explain how a lost season hurts a local economy by naming three Richmond businesses or hourly jobs and how each one's income depends on game days\n\nYou can also show:\n\n- I can explain how lost game-day paychecks could spread into less spending and more unemployment around the city\n- I can identify the terms of a contract by recording the new collective bargaining agreement's final terms on all four issues and explaining what it settles for both sides\n- I can explain what a players' union is and how bargaining together gives athletes power they wouldn't have negotiating one at a time",
